      "This testing is called the elenchus, Greek for refutation or cross-examination. Socrates draws out a definition, gets the partner to agree to other claims, and then shows that those claims contradict the definition. The partner must give up or revise something. The method tests the consistency of a person's beliefs; it does not by itself prove which belief is true.",
      "In Plato's Euthyphro, Euthyphro defines piety as what the gods love. Socrates asks: is something pious because the gods love it, or do the gods love it because it is pious? Either answer causes trouble for the definition. Many dialogues end in aporia, a state of puzzlement in which no definition has survived.",
      "In Plato's Apology, Socrates tells the jury that the oracle at Delphi said no one was wiser than he. After questioning people reputed to be wise, he concluded that they thought they knew things they did not, while he at least did not think he knew what he did not know. He held that the unexamined life is not worth living for a human being."
