      "In 1969 Harry Frankfurt described cases in which a person could not have done otherwise, because a hidden device would have forced the choice, yet the person chooses entirely on their own and the device never acts. Many judge that such a person is morally responsible, which challenges the principle that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise."
