      "Classical conditioning was described by Ivan Pavlov, who noticed that dogs began to salivate before food arrived. Food is an unconditioned stimulus that naturally produces salivation, the unconditioned response. When a neutral stimulus, such as a tone, is repeatedly paired with food, it becomes a conditioned stimulus that produces salivation on its own, now called the conditioned response.",
      "If the conditioned stimulus is presented many times without the unconditioned stimulus, the conditioned response weakens: this is extinction. After a rest, the response may briefly return, which is spontaneous recovery, showing that extinction suppresses rather than erases learning. Stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus can also trigger the response, which is generalization.",
      "Operant conditioning, studied by Edward Thorndike and B. F. Skinner, is learning from the consequences of behaviour. Thorndike's law of effect states that behaviours followed by satisfying consequences become more likely. Reinforcement increases a behaviour; punishment decreases it. Positive means something is added and negative means something is removed: negative reinforcement, such as a seatbelt alarm stopping when you buckle up, increases a behaviour by removing something unpleasant.",
      "Reinforcement can follow every response or only some. Partial schedules are based on the number of responses (ratio) or the time elapsed (interval), each fixed or variable. Behaviour learned on a variable-ratio schedule, like gambling on a slot machine, is especially persistent and resistant to extinction."
