    "passage": [
      "People often judge and decide using heuristics, mental shortcuts that are fast and usually good enough but can lead to systematic errors called cognitive biases. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman described several key heuristics in 1974. Kahneman later described two modes of thinking: a fast, automatic, intuitive System 1 and a slow, effortful, deliberate System 2.",
      "The availability heuristic judges how frequent or likely something is by how easily examples come to mind. Because vivid, recent or heavily reported events are easy to recall, people tend to overestimate the risk of dramatic causes of death such as plane crashes and underestimate common ones.",
      "The representativeness heuristic judges probability by how closely something matches a stereotype or prototype. It leads people to neglect base rates, the overall frequency of a category. Anchoring is the tendency to rely too heavily on an initial number: estimates are pulled toward a starting value even when it is obviously arbitrary, such as a number from a spinning wheel.",
      "Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice and remember information that supports what one already believes and to neglect evidence against it. Framing effects show that logically equivalent choices can be judged differently depending on wording: a treatment described as saving 200 of 600 people is preferred over one described as letting 400 of 600 die, though the outcomes are the same."
