      "In the sensorimotor stage, from birth to about age 2, infants learn through senses and actions. A key achievement is object permanence, the understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight. In the preoperational stage, from about 2 to 7, children use symbols and language and engage in pretend play, but their thinking is egocentric, meaning they struggle to take another person's point of view, and they fail conservation tasks.",
      "Conservation is the understanding that quantity stays the same when its appearance changes: a preoperational child may say a tall thin glass holds more water than a short wide one after watching the same water poured between them. In the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11, children master conservation and reason logically about concrete situations. In the formal operational stage, from about 11 or 12, adolescents can reason abstractly and test hypotheses systematically.",
      "Later research supports the general sequence but suggests Piaget underestimated young children: with simpler tasks, such as measuring how long infants look at impossible events, signs of object knowledge appear earlier than he thought. Development also seems more gradual and more shaped by culture and social interaction than strict stages imply, a point stressed by Lev Vygotsky."
