    "passage": [
      "Social psychology studies how the real or imagined presence of others affects thought and behaviour. Two classic forms of social influence are conformity, changing behaviour to match a group, and obedience, following the orders of an authority figure.",
      "In Solomon Asch's line studies in the 1950s, a participant sat with a group of confederates who, on some trials, unanimously gave an obviously wrong answer about which line matched a standard line. Participants conformed on about a third of these critical trials, and about three quarters conformed at least once. When just one other person gave the correct answer, conformity dropped sharply.",
      "Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard distinguished two reasons people conform. Normative influence is conforming to be liked or accepted and to avoid rejection; informational influence is conforming because one believes the group is more likely to be right, which is stronger when the situation is ambiguous.",
      "In Stanley Milgram's obedience studies, first reported in 1963, participants were told to give increasingly strong electric shocks to a learner, who was in fact an actor receiving no shocks, whenever he made a mistake. In the original study 26 of 40 participants, 65 percent, continued to the maximum 450 volts. Obedience fell when the experimenter was absent or the learner was closer. The studies raised serious ethical concerns about deception and distress, which helped shape modern research ethics rules."
